### SerializationDemo domain — PhpStorm HTTP Client requests
#
# Five strategies for returning a collection from a State Provider, from the
# same 3 source objects (SuggestionItem::samples()). Cases 3a/3b/3c reproduce
# and dissect a real bug (PIC-5442): an ObjectNormalizer with an active
# normalizationContext group filters ALL properties off a class that has no
# matching #[Groups] — silently, with a 200 and a skolem @id, not an error.
# 3c proves the SAME raw object serializes fine when no group is active at
# all — the bug is the active-group-without-declaration combination, not
# "returning a raw object" by itself.
#
# Select an environment first (dropdown top-right, or the gutter icon) —
# PhpStorm never picks one automatically. From http-client.env.json: "dev" or
# "sandbox".

### 1. Manual array — hand-built assoc array in the provider, no serialization
### component involved. title/description/score present, no @type/@id (arrays
### don't get one).
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/serialization-demo/manual
Accept: application/ld+json

> {%
    client.assert(response.body.totalItems === 3, "expected 3 seeded suggestions");
    client.assert(response.body.member[0].title !== undefined, "manual array must carry title");
    client.assert(response.body.member[0]["@id"] === undefined, "plain arrays get no @id");
%}

### 2. Symfony Serializer — NormalizerInterface::normalize() on the same
### SuggestionItemOutput DTO used by case 3b, called directly instead of going
### through API Platform's own normalization pipeline. Same shape as #1.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/serialization-demo/symfony-serializer
Accept: application/ld+json

> {%
    client.assert(response.body.member[0].title !== undefined, "explicit normalize() call must carry title");
    client.assert(response.body.member[0].score === 0.92, "score must round-trip through the normalizer");
%}

### 3a. API Platform, BROKEN — provider returns raw SuggestionItem objects.
### No #[Groups] on the class, but normalizationContext.groups is active on
### the operation -> ObjectNormalizer filters every property. This 200s. It
### does not throw. That's the actual bug: silent data loss, not a crash.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/serialization-demo/api-platform-naive
Accept: application/ld+json

> {%
    client.assert(response.body.totalItems === 3, "the collection itself is still correctly sized");
    client.assert(Object.keys(response.body.member[0]).length === 0, "every property must be stripped — this IS the bug, not a broken assertion");
%}

### 3b. API Platform, CORRECT — output: SuggestionItemOutput::class + matching
### #[Groups] on the DTO. Same normalizationContext.groups as 3a, but now the
### DTO declares the group, so nothing gets filtered.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/serialization-demo/api-platform-dto
Accept: application/ld+json

> {%
    client.assert(response.body.member[0].title !== undefined, "output DTO with matching #[Groups] must survive normalization");
    client.assert(response.body.member[0]["@type"] === "SuggestionItemOutput", "a real API Platform resource object gets @type, unlike cases 1/2");
    client.assert(response.body.member[0]["@id"].includes("/.well-known/genid/"), "no explicit @id on the DTO -> RDF skolemization mints one, still present but harmless once data isn't empty");
%}

### 3c. API Platform, COUNTER-EXAMPLE — the exact same provider as 3a (raw
### SuggestionItem objects, no #[Groups] on the class), but this operation has
### NO normalizationContext at all. No active group -> nothing to filter
### against -> every property survives. Compare directly against 3a: same
### PHP object, same class, only the operation's context differs.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/serialization-demo/no-context
Accept: application/ld+json

> {%
    client.assert(response.body.member[0].title !== undefined, "no active group -> nothing filtered, unlike 3a");
    client.assert(response.body.member[0]["@type"] === "SuggestionItem", "same raw class as 3a — the difference is entirely in the operation's context, not the provider");
    client.assert(response.body.member[0]["@id"].includes("/.well-known/genid/"), "skolemization still applies — it's independent of groups");
%}
